# THE SIBLINGS CI CLONES, BY NAME ONLY: THE COMMITTED `repro.lock` PINS THEM.
#
# `clone-siblings` (lock-store: committed, its default) resolves a BARE entry to
# the revision this commit's own `repro.lock` records for that repo. That is
# the one revision source both CI flavors must share:
#
#   * `ci.yml` (env-flavor: nix) runs the lane against the clones;
#   * `ci-reprobuild.yml` (env-flavor: reprobuild) runs `repro build`, which
#     REFUSES a sibling checkout whose HEAD is not the revision `repro.lock`
#     pins ("cross-repo producer \"isonim\" source revision mismatch").
#
# Until 2026-11-26 each entry here carried an explicit `=<sha>`. That was the
# fallback for a time when no lock pinned these repos (a bare entry was then a
# fatal NEED_LOCK). It became a defect once `repro.lock` did pin them: the
# lock is refreshed with the develop set on every push, the hand-written SHAs
# are not, and the two drifted apart. At agents 29c6c6a the lock pinned isonim
# 38ace5d and nim-everywhere 2f5b67a while these lines still named 37123d2 and
# 23cf930, so CI cloned one pair and `repro build` refused it on every
# platform before building anything. A bare name cannot drift from the lock.
#
# An explicit `name=<40-hex sha>` is still how to clone a repo the lock does
# not pin; `name!=<sha>` acknowledges overriding a revision it does pin. See
# metacraft-github-actions `clone-siblings/clone-siblings.sh`.
isonim
nim-everywhere
